previously-rejected program now accepted, or   a crash where an error was expected. - **Spec drift** — behaviour that now contradicts `SPEC.md` or `docs/GRAMMAR.md`.  ---  ## 6. Auditor contract — the phase-generating loop  Every N phases, the **auditor** runs. It must:  1. Measure the codebase: test count, coverage of `src/*.rs`, gate health,    `todo`/`unimplemented`/`FIXME` counts, largest functions, clippy debt. 2. Diff the language's actual behaviour against `SPEC.md` and `ROADMAP.md`    and list every gap as a concrete, file:line-anchored finding. 3. Turn each finding into a **new phase**: id, title, severity, dependencies,    estimated risk, and an acceptance gate — written into `rbops/phases.json`. 4. Refuse to invent work. A phase with no file:line evidence is not created.  The auditor is the only component permitted to append to `phases.json`.  ---  ## 7. Bootstrap contract — the endgame  **Goal:** Redblue compiles Redblue.  This is a ladder, not a leap. Each rung must be green before the next:  | Stage | Artifact | Definition of done | |---|---|---| | **S0** | Rust `rb` (exists today) | `cargo test` green, `rb` runs all `examples/*.rb` | | **S1** | `rb compile` — Redblue **bytecode compiler** written in Rust | emits `.rbc`; `rb vm file.rbc` runs it | | **S2** | The S1 compiler **rewritten in Redblue** | `rb run bootstrap/compiler.rb` produces byte-identical `.rbc` to S1 for a corpus | | **S3** | S2-compiled compiler compiles itself | fixed point: `stage1.rbc == stage2.rbc` byte-for-byte | | **S4** | S3 binary is the shipped `rb` | `rb` built by `rb` passes the full gate |  Non-negotiable rules for any phase in the S2–S4 range:  - **No shimming.** You may not add a Rust-only fast path that the Redblue path   silently falls back to. If it is not implemented in Redblue, it does not exist. - **Fixed-point is the only proof.** "It compiles" is not done. Byte-identical   output over a ≥200-program corpus is done. - **Self-hosting forbids changing the language to make it easier.** Any grammar   change needed for S2+ must be justified by general language merit in its own phase. - **The gate for every bootstrap phase is the S3 fixed-point test**, plus the   normal four gates. No exceptions, no "next phase will finish it".  ---  ## 8. Communication rules  - Report facts, not narrative. "`rbops/verify.sh` reports `edge_foo: FAILED`" beats "there may be an issue with foo". - When blocked, say exactly what you tried and what the exact error was. - Never fabricate a file:line. If you did not read the line, do not cite it. - Prefer the smallest correct change. A 400-line refactor in a bug-fix phase   is a review BLOCKER.  ---  # YOUR PHASE: phase-101  Project root: /home/runner/work/rbops/rbops/redblue  # phase-101 — Add the text utilities that formatting and diffing need  <!-- GENERATED by rbops/gen-prompts.sh from rbops/phases.json. DO NOT EDIT. -->  | | | |---|---| | Severity | `minor` | | Risk | `low` | | Depends on | phase-090 | | Timeout | 150 min | | Must change | `src/` |  ## You must change `src/`  This phase declares `must_touch: [src/]`. The gate fails the phase unless at least one changed file falls under it. Writing tests, documentation or a report is not enough — if you finish without touching that area, the phase fails on exactly that check.   ## Evidence — verified finding  ``` Redblue has text.uppercase, lowercase, trim, split, join, contains, starts_with, ends_with and replace, and nothing else. There is no wrapping to a width, no unified diff, no indentation-aware dedent, no template substitution, no padding or alignment, and no word handling - so wrapping text to a column, showing what changed between two files, and generating a file from a template are all written by hand in every project that needs them. These are boring, self-contained, and have no dependency on anything else in the backlog. ```  **Re-verify this before you change anything.** If the finding no longer reproduces on `main`, stop and write that